---
import ConverterPage from '../components/ConverterPage.astro';

const faq = [
  {
    q: 'Is this the same as the Chanakya converter?',
    a: 'No. "Chanakya" is a family of fonts from different makers, and they do not all put the same letter on the same key. This page is for one of them, <strong>Abbasi Chanakya New</strong> (Jamal Abbasi, 2010), and was built from that font file itself. Text typed in Walkman Chanakya 905 or another Chanakya cut will not convert correctly here.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Why does it say Beta?',
    a: 'Every key was checked by drawing it in the real font next to the Hindi it converts to. What has not happened yet is a check against a real document typed in this font. A handful of rarely used keys are still a best reading. Until that check is done, read the output before you rely on it.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Does my text get uploaded anywhere?',
    a: 'No. The conversion runs in your browser. Nothing is sent to a server, and once the page has loaded it works offline.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Why does the short-i matra (ि) move?',
    a: 'Abbasi Chanakya New stores letters in the order they are drawn, so ि is typed before its consonant: <strong>fgUnh</strong> is हिन्दी. Unicode wants it after the consonant. The reph (the र् above a letter) is the other way round: it is typed after its syllable, so <strong>deZ</strong> is कर्म.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What happens to English words and numbers?',
    a: 'Numbers, dates, URLs and email addresses are always left alone, and common English words are detected and kept. This font has no Hindi digit keys, so 0-9 stay as they are. Untick the checkbox under the boxes to convert everything.',
  },
];
---

<ConverterPage
  path="/abbasi-chanakya-to-unicode"
  lede="Paste Abbasi Chanakya New text on the left and get Unicode Hindi on the right, with the i-matra and reph in the right order. Nothing is uploaded."
  faq={faq}
>
  <h2>What this converter does</h2>
  <p>
    Abbasi Chanakya New is a legacy Hindi font: its Hindi letters sit on
    ordinary keyboard characters, so a document typed in it shows gibberish like
    <code>Hkkjr</code> on a computer without the font, instead of
    <strong lang="hi">भारत</strong>. This page turns that text into Unicode
    Hindi, which works in every modern app, website and government portal.
  </p>
  <p>
    The box on the left shows the raw code exactly as you pasted it, so it
    will look like jumbled English letters. That is expected. The Hindi appears
    on the right.
  </p>

  <h2>What gets reordered</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>The short-i matra.</strong> Typed before its consonant cluster,
      moved after it: <code>fLFkfr</code> becomes
      <strong lang="hi">स्थिति</strong>. The same goes for the key that gives
      ि with an anusvara: <code>Çlg</code> becomes
      <strong lang="hi">सिंह</strong>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The reph.</strong> Typed after its syllable, moved in front of it.
      This font also has keys that give a reph together with a sign, so
      <code>dhrÆ</code> becomes <strong lang="hi">कीर्ति</strong>.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>How to use it</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Copy the text from your Abbasi Chanakya New document.</li>
    <li>Paste it into the left box. The Unicode appears on the right.</li>
    <li>
      Press <strong>Copy text</strong>, or <strong>Download .docx</strong> for a
      Word file.
    </li>
    <li>Read the output before you use it. This converter is still in beta.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    For the other direction use
    <a href="/unicode-to-abbasi-chanakya">Unicode to Abbasi Chanakya</a>.
    If your document is actually Kruti Dev or DevLys, use
    <a href="/">Kruti Dev to Unicode</a> or
    <a href="/devlys-to-unicode">DevLys to Unicode</a> instead.
  </p>
</ConverterPage>
